1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,267
Here is a strange fact about the history
of cutting tools.

2
00:00:05,617 --> 00:00:11,675
The man who started the modern tool steel
revolution never patented his invention.

3
00:00:12,025 --> 00:00:13,540
He kept it secret.

4
00:00:13,890 --> 00:00:18,199
He bought his ingredients through
middlemen and gave them code names.

5
00:00:18,549 --> 00:00:23,797
And the steel he made still shaped how
metal is cut for generations after him.

6
00:00:24,947 --> 00:00:27,849
His name was Robert Forester Mushet.

7
00:00:28,199 --> 00:00:35,048
In 1868, in a small ironworks in the
Forest of Dean, he made a steel that did

8
00:00:35,048 --> 00:00:38,161
something no steel had done before.

9
00:00:38,511 --> 00:00:40,410
It hardened by itself.

10
00:00:41,560 --> 00:00:45,080
Every blacksmith and toolmaker knew the
old way.

11
00:00:45,430 --> 00:00:50,145
You heated the tool red hot, then plunged
it into water or oil.

12
00:00:50,495 --> 00:00:53,396
The sudden chill locked in the hardness.

13
00:00:53,746 --> 00:00:58,375
It was a violent and risky step, and it
was the heart of the craft.

14
00:00:58,725 --> 00:01:00,624
Mushet's steel skipped it.

15
00:01:00,974 --> 00:01:05,710
You forged the tool, you let it cool in
plain air, and it came out hard.

16
00:01:06,860 --> 00:01:11,020
For a generation, this was the best
cutting steel in the world.

17
00:01:11,370 --> 00:01:16,789
Then it was overtaken by a steel that was
built on the very same idea.

18
00:01:17,139 --> 00:01:21,939
Today almost nobody outside the metal
trades knows his name.

19
00:01:22,289 --> 00:01:24,827
So what happened to Mushet steel?

20
00:01:26,777 --> 00:01:31,901
To see why this mattered, we need to look
at what happens at the tip of a cutting

21
00:01:31,901 --> 00:01:32,217
tool.

22
00:01:33,367 --> 00:01:38,146
A lathe tool does not slice metal the way
a knife slices bread.

23
00:01:38,496 --> 00:01:44,000
It pushes into the work and forces a layer
of metal to shear away as a chip.

24
00:01:44,350 --> 00:01:48,595
That chip slides over the face of the tool
under huge pressure.

25
00:01:48,945 --> 00:01:54,151
The friction and the bending of the metal
turn nearly all of that effort into heat.

26
00:01:54,501 --> 00:01:59,087
And almost all of that heat collects in a
tiny patch right at the edge.

27
00:02:00,237 --> 00:02:05,208
For most of the nineteenth century, tools
were made of plain carbon steel.

28
00:02:05,558 --> 00:02:09,953
Carbon steel can be made very hard by
heating and quenching.

29
00:02:10,303 --> 00:02:12,777
But that hardness has a weakness.

30
00:02:13,127 --> 00:02:16,583
Heat the tool again, and the hardness
drains away.

31
00:02:16,933 --> 00:02:21,797
So a cutting edge in a carbon steel tool
is always fighting itself.

32
00:02:22,147 --> 00:02:27,395
The harder you push the cut, the hotter
the edge gets, and the faster it softens.

33
00:02:28,545 --> 00:02:31,191
The machinist's answer was patience.

34
00:02:31,541 --> 00:02:32,927
Take light cuts.

35
00:02:33,277 --> 00:02:34,621
Run slowly.

36
00:02:34,971 --> 00:02:36,273
Stop often.

37
00:02:36,623 --> 00:02:41,252
Keep the tool cool and sharp, and never
let the edge change color.

38
00:02:42,402 --> 00:02:46,477
That habit set the pace of the entire
industrial world.

39
00:02:46,827 --> 00:02:51,730
It limited how fast a shaft could be
turned, how fast a cylinder could be

40
00:02:51,730 --> 00:02:55,424
bored, and how many parts a shop could
finish in a day.

41
00:02:55,774 --> 00:02:59,657
Any steel that could cut faster would be
worth a fortune.

42
00:03:00,007 --> 00:03:01,500
Mushet found one.

43
00:03:01,850 --> 00:03:04,495
He just never collected the fortune.

44
00:03:06,445 --> 00:03:13,568
Robert Forester Mushet was born on the
eighth of April, 1811, in Coleford, in the

45
00:03:13,568 --> 00:03:16,557
Forest of Dean in Gloucestershire.

46
00:03:16,907 --> 00:03:19,766
He was the youngest son of Scottish
parents.

47
00:03:20,116 --> 00:03:25,402
His father, David Mushet, was an
ironmaster, and the family business was

48
00:03:25,402 --> 00:03:25,769
iron.

49
00:03:26,919 --> 00:03:35,111
David built the Darkhill Ironworks around
1818 or 1819.

50
00:03:35,461 --> 00:03:40,731
Robert took over its management in 1845.

51
00:03:41,081 --> 00:03:46,499
In 1848 he moved to the Forest Steel
Works.

52
00:03:46,849 --> 00:03:52,161
There, by one account, he ran more than
ten thousand experiments in ten years.

53
00:03:53,311 --> 00:03:55,253
Think about what that means.

54
00:03:55,603 --> 00:03:59,635
He was not a university professor with a
laboratory budget.

55
00:03:59,985 --> 00:04:05,349
He was a working ironmaster in a rural
valley, melting small batches, testing

56
00:04:05,349 --> 00:04:08,902
them, writing down the results, and doing
it again.

57
00:04:09,252 --> 00:04:11,044
Ten thousand times.

58
00:04:12,194 --> 00:04:17,079
It was the kind of patient, practical work
that built the nineteenth century.

59
00:04:17,429 --> 00:04:22,613
And it led him first to a problem that had
nothing to do with cutting tools at all.

60
00:04:22,963 --> 00:04:26,825
It was the problem that was holding back
the age of steel.

61
00:04:28,775 --> 00:04:36,099
In 1856, Henry Bessemer described his new
steelmaking process to the British

62
00:04:36,099 --> 00:04:39,953
Association, at a meeting in Cheltenham.

63
00:04:40,303 --> 00:04:42,330
The idea was bold.

64
00:04:42,680 --> 00:04:48,184
Blow air through molten pig iron, and the
oxygen burns out the silicon, the

65
00:04:48,184 --> 00:04:50,019
manganese and the carbon.

66
00:04:50,369 --> 00:04:54,721
Bessemer was after cheap steel, and this
was a way to get it.

67
00:04:55,871 --> 00:04:57,321
But there was a catch.

68
00:04:57,671 --> 00:05:00,530
The metal that came out was often poor.

69
00:05:00,880 --> 00:05:03,184
It could not be worked well when hot.

70
00:05:04,334 --> 00:05:05,934
Mushet had a fix.

71
00:05:06,284 --> 00:05:11,148
He showed that you could blow the iron
until nearly all the carbon was gone.

72
00:05:11,498 --> 00:05:16,426
Then you added back a measured amount of a
material called spiegeleisen.

73
00:05:16,776 --> 00:05:20,104
The name is German for looking-glass iron.

74
00:05:20,454 --> 00:05:24,678
It is an alloy of iron and manganese, with
a little carbon.

75
00:05:25,028 --> 00:05:30,435
The addition restored the carbon in a
controlled way, and the manganese made the

76
00:05:30,435 --> 00:05:31,449
steel workable.

77
00:05:32,599 --> 00:05:36,589
Bessemer had not solved this quality
problem himself.

78
00:05:37,739 --> 00:05:42,453
Mushet's patent on the method lapsed,
because he could not pay the fees.

79
00:05:42,803 --> 00:05:44,702
Bessemer took it over.

80
00:05:45,052 --> 00:05:51,111
By 1866, Mushet was destitute and in poor
health.

81
00:05:51,461 --> 00:05:56,679
That year, his sixteen-year-old daughter
Mary travelled to London alone and

82
00:05:56,679 --> 00:06:01,829
confronted Bessemer, arguing that his
success rested on her father's work.

83
00:06:02,979 --> 00:06:08,291
Bessemer then agreed to pay Mushet a
pension of three hundred pounds a year.

84
00:06:08,641 --> 00:06:11,094
That was a very considerable sum.

85
00:06:11,444 --> 00:06:16,116
He paid it for more than twenty years,
possibly to head off legal action.

86
00:06:17,266 --> 00:06:24,391
In 1857, Mushet also made some of the
first durable steel rails.

87
00:06:24,741 --> 00:06:31,583
One rail at Derby Midland station was
still in good condition in 1863, even

88
00:06:31,583 --> 00:06:36,965
though about seven hundred trains passed
over it every day.

89
00:06:38,915 --> 00:06:44,145
Two years later, Mushet came back to the
experiment that mattered most for our

90
00:06:44,145 --> 00:06:44,547
story.

91
00:06:45,697 --> 00:06:50,775
A manufacturer in Glasgow had asked him to
produce a new cast steel.

92
00:06:51,125 --> 00:06:54,794
The steel he made was too brittle for
cutting tools.

93
00:06:55,144 --> 00:06:58,536
So Mushet kept experimenting with other
alloys.

94
00:06:58,886 --> 00:07:03,622
One of them was made from tungsten ore and
manganese-rich iron ore.

95
00:07:03,972 --> 00:07:07,364
It held an edge better than any carbon
steel.

96
00:07:07,714 --> 00:07:12,386
And, unexpectedly, it hardened when it
cooled in air after forging.

97
00:07:13,536 --> 00:07:19,125
Tungsten was the main alloying element,
somewhere between four and twelve percent.

98
00:07:19,475 --> 00:07:24,677
Manganese ran from about two to four
percent, and carbon from about one and a

99
00:07:24,677 --> 00:07:26,771
half to two and a half percent.

100
00:07:27,121 --> 00:07:32,966
A typical sample held about nine percent
tungsten, two and a half percent manganese

101
00:07:32,966 --> 00:07:35,783
and one and eight tenths percent carbon.

102
00:07:36,933 --> 00:07:40,538
Compare that with the carbon steel in a
normal shop.

103
00:07:40,888 --> 00:07:45,944
It was mostly iron and carbon, with only
small amounts of anything else.

104
00:07:46,294 --> 00:07:49,494
Mushet's steel was a different kind of
material.

105
00:07:49,844 --> 00:07:52,084
It was a true alloy steel.

106
00:07:53,234 --> 00:07:55,943
And he told no one how to make it.

107
00:07:56,293 --> 00:07:57,893
He did not patent it.

108
00:07:58,243 --> 00:08:02,915
Ingredients were bought through
intermediaries and called by code words.

109
00:08:03,265 --> 00:08:07,212
Mixing was done at his steelworks in the
Forest of Dean.

110
00:08:07,562 --> 00:08:12,771
The mixed batches were shipped in barrels
to Samuel Osborn and Company in Sheffield,

111
00:08:12,771 --> 00:08:14,197
where they were melted.

112
00:08:15,347 --> 00:08:18,760
It was sold as Robert Mushet's Special
Steel.

113
00:08:19,110 --> 00:08:21,713
Some called it self-hardening steel.

114
00:08:22,063 --> 00:08:24,665
Others called it air-hardening steel.

115
00:08:26,615 --> 00:08:31,884
Now let us look at the engineering,
because this is where the idea is easy to

116
00:08:31,884 --> 00:08:32,226
miss.

117
00:08:33,376 --> 00:08:37,792
In a plain carbon steel, hardness is
something you have to trap.

118
00:08:38,142 --> 00:08:41,449
You heat the steel until its structure
changes.

119
00:08:41,799 --> 00:08:46,873
Then you cool it so fast that the
structure has no time to settle into its

120
00:08:46,873 --> 00:08:47,559
soft form.

121
00:08:47,909 --> 00:08:49,466
Water does that.

122
00:08:49,816 --> 00:08:51,160
Oil does that.

123
00:08:51,510 --> 00:08:52,875
Air does not.

124
00:08:53,225 --> 00:08:58,409
Air is far too slow, so carbon steel left
to cool in air stays soft.

125
00:08:59,559 --> 00:09:02,247
Mushet's steel behaved differently.

126
00:09:02,597 --> 00:09:07,541
Its large amounts of tungsten and
manganese changed how it responded to

127
00:09:07,541 --> 00:09:11,301
cooling, so that ordinary air was enough
to harden it.

128
00:09:11,651 --> 00:09:14,830
The tool hardened as it cooled off the
forge.

129
00:09:15,980 --> 00:09:18,199
That mattered in two ways.

130
00:09:19,349 --> 00:09:21,994
First, it made the tool easier to make.

131
00:09:22,344 --> 00:09:27,165
A toolmaker could forge the shape and let
it cool, without a water quench.

132
00:09:27,515 --> 00:09:30,033
Mushet's steel removed that step.

133
00:09:31,183 --> 00:09:35,513
Second, and more important, it made the
tool better at its work.

134
00:09:35,863 --> 00:09:40,770
One modern account says it was harder than
ordinary water-quenched steel.

135
00:09:41,120 --> 00:09:43,360
It was also non-magnetic.

136
00:09:44,510 --> 00:09:47,454
The steel came to the shop already hard.

137
00:09:47,804 --> 00:09:51,345
It was made for one job, and that job was
cutting.

138
00:09:53,295 --> 00:09:56,559
We know how good it was because someone
measured it.

139
00:09:57,709 --> 00:10:05,744
In 1894, the American engineer Frederick
Winslow Taylor tested Mushet steel against

140
00:10:05,744 --> 00:10:07,971
high-carbon tool steel.

141
00:10:08,321 --> 00:10:14,017
On hard tire-steel forgings, it cut
forty-one to forty-seven percent faster.

142
00:10:14,367 --> 00:10:18,271
On mild steels, it cut about ninety
percent faster.

143
00:10:18,621 --> 00:10:23,940
And when a stream of water was used as a
cutting fluid, the speed could go up by

144
00:10:23,940 --> 00:10:25,469
another thirty percent.

145
00:10:26,619 --> 00:10:29,456
Think about a change of ninety percent.

146
00:10:29,806 --> 00:10:33,262
A job that took ten hours now took about
five.

147
00:10:33,612 --> 00:10:36,129
No new machine had been built.

148
00:10:36,479 --> 00:10:39,103
No new motor had been installed.

149
00:10:39,453 --> 00:10:42,205
Only the steel at the tip had changed.

150
00:10:43,355 --> 00:10:48,134
Taylor would become the most famous name
in the story of cutting speed.

151
00:10:48,484 --> 00:10:50,596
But he was not starting from nothing.

152
00:10:50,946 --> 00:10:52,781
He started from Mushet.

153
00:10:54,731 --> 00:11:00,397
Mushet steel needed someone to make it at
scale, and that someone was a Sheffield

154
00:11:00,397 --> 00:11:00,747
firm.

155
00:11:01,897 --> 00:11:09,508
Samuel Osborn had set up his company in
1851 at the Clyde Works in Sheffield,

156
00:11:09,508 --> 00:11:10,793
making files.

157
00:11:11,143 --> 00:11:17,964
In 1870, according to the Science Museum
Group, Osborn became the exclusive

158
00:11:17,964 --> 00:11:21,148
producer of Mushet's special steel.

159
00:11:22,298 --> 00:11:23,663
The firm grew.

160
00:11:24,013 --> 00:11:29,496
One account says it virtually monopolised
the world's manufacture of tool steel.

161
00:11:29,846 --> 00:11:35,731
That is a big claim, and it comes from a
single company history, so treat it as a

162
00:11:35,731 --> 00:11:37,185
company's own pride.

163
00:11:37,535 --> 00:11:39,732
But the direction is clear.

164
00:11:40,082 --> 00:11:42,813
Osborn tied its name to Mushet's steel.

165
00:11:43,963 --> 00:11:47,184
From Sheffield it went out to the tool
trade.

166
00:11:47,534 --> 00:11:54,105
It was the steel Taylor himself put to the
test in 1894.

167
00:11:55,255 --> 00:12:03,369
In 1876, the Iron and Steel Institute gave
Mushet its highest honor, the Bessemer

168
00:12:03,369 --> 00:12:04,471
Gold Medal.

169
00:12:04,821 --> 00:12:09,749
It was a late recognition for a man who
had once been left destitute.

170
00:12:10,899 --> 00:12:17,299
Another of his ideas, from 1861, is still
around today.

171
00:12:17,649 --> 00:12:19,441
It is called the dozzle.

172
00:12:19,791 --> 00:12:25,870
It is a heated clay cone placed on top of
an ingot mould, so that molten steel can

173
00:12:25,870 --> 00:12:28,687
feed the shrinkage as the ingot cools.

174
00:12:29,037 --> 00:12:32,919
Today the idea is called a hot top, or a
feeder head.

175
00:12:34,069 --> 00:12:41,216
Mushet died in Cheltenham on the
twenty-ninth of January, 1891.

176
00:12:41,566 --> 00:12:43,678
He was seventy-nine years old.

177
00:12:45,628 --> 00:12:48,700
Mushet did not live to see what came next.

178
00:12:49,050 --> 00:12:52,805
And what came next was not a different
material at all.

179
00:12:53,155 --> 00:12:56,141
It was Mushet steel, treated differently.

180
00:12:57,291 --> 00:13:04,558
In 1898, Frederick Winslow Taylor joined
the Bethlehem Steel Company in

181
00:13:04,558 --> 00:13:05,889
Pennsylvania.

182
00:13:06,239 --> 00:13:14,773
Between 1898 and 1900, he and a colleague,
Maunsel White, ran a huge program of tests

183
00:13:14,773 --> 00:13:17,183
on tungsten tool steels.

184
00:13:17,533 --> 00:13:21,607
They worked on Mushet steel and other
self-hardening steels.

185
00:13:22,757 --> 00:13:28,375
They found that if you heated the steel to
a temperature close to its melting point,

186
00:13:28,375 --> 00:13:33,659
far above what the industry normally used,
the result was a more durable metal.

187
00:13:34,009 --> 00:13:36,718
It kept its hardness up to red heat.

188
00:13:37,068 --> 00:13:42,423
One history of the steel says the key
change was adding about three point eight

189
00:13:42,423 --> 00:13:46,625
percent chromium to a steel with about
eight percent tungsten.

190
00:13:47,775 --> 00:13:49,802
The effect was dramatic.

191
00:13:50,152 --> 00:13:55,656
Cutting speed rose from about thirty
surface feet per minute to about ninety.

192
00:13:56,006 --> 00:13:58,203
That is three times faster.

193
00:13:59,353 --> 00:14:05,176
At the Paris Exposition of 1900, a
demonstration of cutting tools made from

194
00:14:05,176 --> 00:14:07,737
the new steel caused a sensation.

195
00:14:08,087 --> 00:14:11,693
This was the start of what we call
high-speed steel.

196
00:14:12,843 --> 00:14:14,656
Notice what happened.

197
00:14:15,006 --> 00:14:18,974
Mushet had made the first steel that
hardened without a quench.

198
00:14:19,324 --> 00:14:24,665
Taylor and White took that kind of steel,
changed the alloy, and heat treated it at

199
00:14:24,665 --> 00:14:27,303
a much higher temperature than was usual.

200
00:14:27,653 --> 00:14:31,813
The same idea, pushed further, cut three
times as fast.

201
00:14:32,963 --> 00:14:36,035
For Taylor and White, it was also a
business.

202
00:14:36,385 --> 00:14:41,846
The two men received one hundred thousand
dollars for the English patents alone.

203
00:14:42,196 --> 00:14:45,631
The American patent was eventually
nullified.

204
00:14:47,581 --> 00:14:51,293
So why did Mushet steel disappear from the
lathe?

205
00:14:52,443 --> 00:14:58,117
The honest answer is that the sources give
us the shape of it, but not every detail.

206
00:14:58,467 --> 00:15:00,494
Here is what we can say.

207
00:15:01,644 --> 00:15:03,927
First, the better steel arrived.

208
00:15:04,277 --> 00:15:09,900
Once Taylor and White's method was known,
a tool that held its hardness to red heat

209
00:15:09,900 --> 00:15:14,303
and cut about three times as fast made the
older steel look slow.

210
00:15:15,453 --> 00:15:17,992
Second, the idea kept improving.

211
00:15:18,342 --> 00:15:24,776
In 1910, a steel called T1, a tungsten
grade, was made as one of the first

212
00:15:24,776 --> 00:15:27,558
mass-produced high-speed steels.

213
00:15:27,908 --> 00:15:32,452
It was patented by the Crucible Steel
Company in New Jersey.

214
00:15:32,802 --> 00:15:38,878
Later, molybdenum was substituted for
tungsten, and a steel called M2 took over

215
00:15:38,878 --> 00:15:41,186
as the most widely used grade.

216
00:15:41,536 --> 00:15:48,856
Cobalt was added to some grades for more
heat resistance, to give steels like M35

217
00:15:48,856 --> 00:15:49,579
and M42.

218
00:15:50,729 --> 00:15:54,291
Third, Mushet had chosen not to patent his
steel.

219
00:15:54,641 --> 00:15:58,524
He relied on secrecy, and secrecy does not
last.

220
00:15:58,874 --> 00:16:04,076
The composition of his steel has since
been analyzed, although the exact

221
00:16:04,076 --> 00:16:07,471
production process is still said to be
unknown.

222
00:16:08,621 --> 00:16:11,395
None of this is a story of failure.

223
00:16:11,745 --> 00:16:16,971
It is a story of a good idea being passed
on until it became ordinary.

224
00:16:18,921 --> 00:16:22,697
Mushet steel itself is long gone from the
shop floor.

225
00:16:23,047 --> 00:16:25,479
But its descendants are everywhere.

226
00:16:26,629 --> 00:16:31,835
High-speed steel is still one of the most
common tool materials in the world.

227
00:16:32,185 --> 00:16:36,430
The grade M2 is used for drill bits, taps
and reamers.

228
00:16:36,780 --> 00:16:42,037
Many of the drills in a modern toolbox are
made from a steel that traces back to

229
00:16:42,037 --> 00:16:44,993
Taylor and White, and through them to
Mushet.

230
00:16:46,143 --> 00:16:48,682
The name itself lasted too.

231
00:16:49,032 --> 00:16:54,962
Samuel Osborn and Company of Sheffield
sold a range of high-speed steels under

232
00:16:54,962 --> 00:16:56,179
the Mushet name.

233
00:16:56,529 --> 00:17:01,713
There was Double Mushet ND, which matches
the American grade T1.

234
00:17:02,063 --> 00:17:06,159
There was Triple Mushet GZ, which matches
T4.

235
00:17:06,509 --> 00:17:10,391
There was Mushet MKK, which matches M2.

236
00:17:10,741 --> 00:17:15,179
And there was Mushet Special VG, which
matches M15.

237
00:17:16,329 --> 00:17:21,626
So even after the original secret steel
was gone, a customer could still buy a

238
00:17:21,626 --> 00:17:24,478
high-speed steel with Mushet's name on it.

239
00:17:24,828 --> 00:17:26,577
It was a different steel.

240
00:17:26,927 --> 00:17:28,677
But it carried his name.

241
00:17:29,827 --> 00:17:32,835
His other work is still in use as well.

242
00:17:33,185 --> 00:17:38,518
The hot top on a steel ingot mould is a
direct descendant of the dozzle.

243
00:17:38,868 --> 00:17:44,311
And his spiegeleisen step improved the
quality of Bessemer steel in the first

244
00:17:44,311 --> 00:17:44,735
place.

245
00:17:46,685 --> 00:17:52,355
Mushet steel teaches a few things that
every machinist and every engineer should

246
00:17:52,355 --> 00:17:53,277
keep in mind.

247
00:17:54,427 --> 00:17:58,480
The first is that the great step is often
the first one.

248
00:17:58,830 --> 00:18:01,625
Mushet did not make the fastest steel.

249
00:18:01,975 --> 00:18:07,147
He made the first steel that hardened by
itself, and everything that followed grew

250
00:18:07,147 --> 00:18:07,777
from that.

251
00:18:08,127 --> 00:18:10,730
Taylor and White improved on it.

252
00:18:11,080 --> 00:18:13,000
They did not invent it.

253
00:18:14,150 --> 00:18:18,950
The second is that a secret is a poor way
to protect an idea.

254
00:18:19,300 --> 00:18:23,588
Mushet kept the recipe locked away, and it
did not make him rich.

255
00:18:23,938 --> 00:18:28,706
Taylor and White were paid one hundred
thousand dollars for their English

256
00:18:28,706 --> 00:18:31,383
patents, and still lost the American one.

257
00:18:31,733 --> 00:18:35,061
There is no easy lesson about patents
here.

258
00:18:35,411 --> 00:18:40,245
But it is a reminder that the person who
makes the breakthrough is rarely the

259
00:18:40,245 --> 00:18:42,003
person who gets paid for it.

260
00:18:43,153 --> 00:18:45,308
The third is the simplest.

261
00:18:45,658 --> 00:18:49,391
A cutting edge is a hot, small,
unforgiving place.

262
00:18:49,741 --> 00:18:54,855
Every material that has ever worked there
has won by staying hard when the heat

263
00:18:54,855 --> 00:18:55,373
arrived.

264
00:18:56,523 --> 00:19:02,020
Robert Mushet ran more than ten thousand
experiments in a Forest of Dean

265
00:19:02,020 --> 00:19:02,859
steelworks.

266
00:19:03,209 --> 00:19:10,833
He solved a key quality problem in the
Bessemer process, and by 1866 he was

267
00:19:10,833 --> 00:19:11,849
destitute.

268
00:19:12,199 --> 00:19:15,911
Then he made the first tool steel that
hardened in air.

269
00:19:16,261 --> 00:19:19,973
The steel in a modern drill bit owes him a
debt.

270
00:19:21,123 --> 00:19:23,854
That is what happened to Mushet steel.

271
00:19:25,004 --> 00:19:30,730
If you want to see what came after it, how
the Taylor and White heat treatment really

272
00:19:30,730 --> 00:19:35,985
worked, and why Paris in 1900 stunned the
engineers who saw it, stay with this

273
00:19:35,985 --> 00:19:36,524
channel.

274
00:19:36,874 --> 00:19:41,759
And tell us in the comments which
forgotten tool we should open up next.

